---
import Article from "../../../layouts/Article.astro";
import KeyTakeaway from "../../../components/KeyTakeaway.astro";
import InfoBox from "../../../components/InfoBox.astro";
import ComparisonTable from "../../../components/ComparisonTable.astro";
import QuickFact from "../../../components/QuickFact.astro";
---

<Article
  title="Flip 7 Strategy: When to Hit and When to Stay"
  description="The card counts are wildly uneven — here's how that changes every decision you make."
  game="flip-7"
  gameTitle="Flip 7"
  category="Strategy"
  publishDate="2026-08-09"
>
  <p>
    Flip 7 feels like a coin flip, and for the first few plays it is. Then you
    notice something: the deck is deliberately lopsided, and once you can see the
    shape of it, "hit or stay" stops being a guess.
  </p>

  <KeyTakeaway answer="Your bust risk depends on which numbers you hold, not how many. A row of low cards is almost unbustable and should chase the bonus. A row of high cards should bank early." />

  <h2>The Deck Is Not Uniform</h2>

  <p>
    Every number card appears as many times as its value. One 1. Two 2s. Ten 10s.
    Twelve 12s. That single design decision drives the entire game.
  </p>

  <p>
    It means the card that scores you the most is also the card most likely to
    kill you later. Holding a 12 leaves eleven more 12s in the deck hunting you.
    Holding a 1 leaves none.
  </p>

  <div class="my-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
    <QuickFact label="Copies of 12" value="12" icon="💀" />
    <QuickFact label="Copies of 1" value="1" icon="🛡️" />
    <QuickFact label="Number cards total" value="79" icon="🃏" />
  </div>

  <h2>What Your Row Is Actually Worth</h2>

  <p>
    Here's the same row size at three different heights, with the chance that the
    next number card busts you. "Live duplicates" means copies of your numbers
    still unseen in the deck.
  </p>

  <ComparisonTable title="Bust risk on the next number card">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Your row</th>
          <th>Points</th>
          <th>Live duplicates</th>
          <th>Bust risk</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>1, 2, 3</td>
          <td>6</td>
          <td>3</td>
          <td><strong class="text-emerald-700">~4%</strong></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>5, 6, 7</td>
          <td>18</td>
          <td>15</td>
          <td><strong class="text-amber-700">~20%</strong></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>10, 11, 12</td>
          <td>33</td>
          <td>30</td>
          <td><strong class="text-red-700">~39%</strong></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>8, 9, 10, 11, 12</td>
          <td>50</td>
          <td>45</td>
          <td><strong class="text-red-700">~61%</strong></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>0, 1, 2, 3, 4</td>
          <td>10</td>
          <td>6</td>
          <td><strong class="text-emerald-700">~8%</strong></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </ComparisonTable>

  <InfoBox type="rule" title="Read the numbers, not the count">
    Two players both showing five cards can be in completely different positions:
    one at 8% risk, the other at 61%. Card count tells you nothing on its own.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>The Two Game Plans</h2>

  <div class="my-6 grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
    <div class="p-5 bg-emerald-50 rounded-xl border border-emerald-200">
      <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-emerald-800 mb-3">🛡️ Low row → chase the bonus</h4>
      <ul class="text-sm text-emerald-700/70 space-y-2">
        <li>• Duplicates barely exist, so keep hitting</li>
        <li>• The row is worth little without the +15</li>
        <li>• A 0–6 Flip 7 scores 21 + 15 = <strong>36</strong></li>
        <li>• You're the player who should take Flip Three volunteers</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="p-5 bg-red-50 rounded-xl border border-red-200">
      <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-red-800 mb-3">💰 High row → bank early</h4>
      <ul class="text-sm text-red-700/70 space-y-2">
        <li>• Three or four high cards is already a good round</li>
        <li>• Every extra card is a coin flip for ~10 points</li>
        <li>• Risking 40 banked points to win 11 is a bad trade</li>
        <li>• Stay, and let the greedy players hand you the round</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The mistake most new players make is treating Flip 7 as one game. It's two.
    The cards you're dealt in the first three flips decide which one you're
    playing, and pushing a high row toward the bonus is how good rounds turn into
    zeroes.
  </p>

  <h2>Modifiers Change the Maths</h2>

  <p>
    A +10 or a ×2 raises what you stand to lose without raising your bust risk at
    all. If you're holding a ×2 on a decent row, your stay threshold should drop
    sharply — you now have far more to protect.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="warning" title="A bust takes the modifiers too">
    Busting discards everything in front of you, including that +10 you were so
    pleased about. Modifiers are a reason to stop, not a reason to push.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Playing the Table, Not the Cards</h2>

  <p>
    Because the game ends at 200 and only cumulative totals matter, correct play
    changes with the score:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>You're comfortably ahead:</strong> stay early and often. Small banked rounds run out the clock while others bust.</li>
    <li><strong>Someone is close to 200:</strong> safe play is now losing play. Push for a Flip 7, because a 25-point round doesn't matter any more.</li>
    <li><strong>You're holding a Freeze:</strong> the target is whoever is deepest into a low row, not whoever has the most points. They're the one about to score 36.</li>
    <li><strong>You're holding a Flip Three:</strong> use it on a deep high row. Against an empty row it's a gift.</li>
  </ul>

  <InfoBox type="tip" title="The single best habit to build">
    Before you say "hit", name the numbers that would bust you and roughly how
    many are left. It takes two seconds, it's the entire skill of the game, and
    it turns Flip 7 from a luck game into a game you can be genuinely good at.
  </InfoBox>

  <p>
    Percentages above count only number cards. In practice roughly one card in
    six is a modifier or action card that can't bust you, so real-world risk per
    card dealt is a little lower than the table shows — but the relative picture
    is exactly right.
  </p>
</Article>
